{
  "title": "⭕ Constructing Tangents to a Circle",
  "hook": "Given a circle and a point outside it, can you draw the lines that 'just touch' the circle? This classical construction uses a beautiful property: the angle in a semicircle is 90°!",
  "explanation": "<p><b>Two types of tangent constructions:</b></p><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Situation</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Method</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\"># Tangents</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Point ON the circle</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Draw radius, then perpendicular</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Point OUTSIDE the circle</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Use semicircle construction</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">2</td></tr></table><p><b>Construction: Tangent from an external point P to circle with centre O, radius r:</b></p><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#2E7D32;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Join OP</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#2E7D32;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Find midpoint M of OP</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Use perpendicular bisector</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#2E7D32;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Draw circle with centre M, radius MO = MP</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">This circle passes through both O and P</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#2E7D32;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Let this circle intersect the given circle at A and B</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#2E7D32;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">5</div><div><strong>PA and PB are the required tangents!</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div></div><div style=\"background:#F5F5F5;border-left:3px solid #4A90D9;border-radius:6px;padding:8px 12px;margin:6px 0;font-size:0.9rem\"><b>Why?</b> ∠OAP = 90° (angle in semicircle of the new circle). Since OA is radius and ∠OAP = 90°, PA must be tangent to the original circle!</div><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> For a tangent at a point ON the circle: just draw the radius to that point, then construct a perpendicular to the radius at that point.</div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Example:</b> Draw a circle of radius 3 cm. From point P at 7 cm from centre, construct tangents. Find the tangent length.<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Draw circle, centre O, r = 3 cm. Mark P at 7 cm from O</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Find midpoint M of OP (at 3.5 cm from each)</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Draw circle with centre M, radius 3.5 cm</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Mark intersection points A, B with original circle</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">5</div><div><strong>Join PA, PB — these are tangents</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">6</div><div><strong>Tangent length = √(OP² − r²) = √(49−9) = √40 = 2√10 ≈ 6.32 cm</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div></div>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Draw a circle of radius 4 cm. Mark a point P at 10 cm from centre. If you construct tangents PA and PB, what is the length PA? What angle does OP make with PA?",
    "hint": "PA = √(OP²−r²). ∠OAP = 90°. Find ∠OPA using trigonometry.",
    "answer": "PA = √(100−16) = √84 = 2√21 ≈ 9.17 cm. sin(∠OPA) = OA/OP = 4/10 = 2/5. ∠OPA = arcsin(0.4) ≈ 23.6°."
  },
  "fun_fact": "This construction is over 2300 years old — Euclid described it in Book III of his Elements!"
}